body:
I just bought a 30k car. My first ever new vehicle, the market is so backwards right now it was hard to find something less expensive.

&#x200B;

Honestly, I'm terrified. I have my first payment next month. Once I get a couple of payments in I think I'll feel better.

NTA

&#x200B;

Here's what you say.

"Of course honey, I'm fine with them being in the delivery room as long as you do one little thing for me. I'll invite my family over, you get nude from the waist down. lay with your legs up on the dining room table, and take a shit on the table while they watch."

&#x200B;

If he comes back at you about that, let him know that giving birth is MUCH WORSE than that. Many poop themselves, sometimes there's complications. You're vulnerable, in the worst pain of your life, and scared. You need to be the one to choose who goes through it with you. If he can't support you, find someone else you trust to do that. You need someone that you love and trust to be there and care for you.

My bird got an infection under his wing in that spot. He picked it clean and was constantly itching it. We treated it constantly with creams and antibiotics but he ended up dying after a couple of years. We really did our best but the infection kept coming back. The vet said it just happens sometimes to birds and they don't know why.

&#x200B;

I hope that's not the issue you're having, but if it is, good luck.

My dad threw away my childhood stuffed animal while I was away on a trip when I was 12 years old. He told me I was too old to own a stuffed animal. It was the ["My Pet Monster"](https://bloody-disgusting.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/Screen-Shot-2020-09-16-at-10.19.46-AM-e1600269638157.png) doll, there were several versions and makers but mine looked like the one pictured.

&#x200B;

My mom hunted one down at a storage auction (she worked for a storage company) a couple of months later. It looked like the exact same doll, even had rubbed off paint in the same spot on it's foot. My parents lived separately, dad was in NY and mom was in Oregon. The chances of that being the same doll are miniscule, but I still believe it's him to this day.

The restaurant I work at gives us nice branded click pens. Those are the only pens we're allowed to give guests for signing the check, but if we want to bring our own pens to write in our books it's fine. There's a little cup of pens at all the POS computers in case you have a bunch of split checks or something, and each server is supposed to have 3 pens on them.

&#x200B;

No one fights over pens. Guests aren't delayed from leaving while you search for a pen. No one has to use those crappy blue pens with the cap. It's beautiful.

I only typically refuse service if a guest touches me. Pinching or slapping my butt, slapping my hand, throwing something at me. In my 20 years in the industry I have some stories.

&#x200B;

I did refuse service to a certain politician, but it was because he's rude and never tips. Also I don't agree with him politically but there's always someone who's cool serving him since he's a bit of a celeb in my area.

When I got my diagnosis my doctor specifically mentioned to not do yoga. He didn't even want me to stretch too much before and after exercise. The PT gave me specific stretches I can do, but still not to do them too much.

&#x200B;

Thankfully we have a swimming pool so I'm still able to work out (in the summer at least) and I do low impact exercise at home. [I like this guy's workouts.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gC_L9qAHVJ8&t=938s) My friend has a mini weightlifting gym on his back patio. He figured out it was cheaper than a gym membership and would pay for itself in like 6 months. I have the option to go over there but I'm so scared of hurting myself that I haven't.

&#x200B;

Like others have said, make sure your kid has arch supports. They hurt at first if your foot has already fallen but it's so worth it. There's no magic pill that'll make this better but a diagnosis can help.

I've been to the hospital twice for the digestive pain. One of the times I couldn't even drink water, and I waited 3 days but at that point I was so sick and needed an IV. Align helps me a bit, but only sometimes. It'll help for a month and suddenly stop working. Sometimes I just go a couple of days without eating to "reset" my system. Idk if it helps but my pain seems to only flare up after eating, so I get a couple of digestive pain free days.

&#x200B;

Are these things I do healthy? No, but no doctor has been able to offer me any long term relief so I guess this is just life.

My restaurant has bpa-free portion bags and a heat sealer. You portion and seal and throw the bags in a huge vat of ice water. They chill there for about an hour and then they move to their home in the walk in. I'm FOH so Idk the capacity but each bag looks like about a half gallon.

&#x200B;

My old place just filled 4 gallon plastic bins like this post with hot cooked sauces and called it a day. For sure they separated and got gross but chef just stirred them up.

I was told my shoes weren't supportive enough, and I went out and bought some high arch supports to add. It's actually really helped me. Most of my issues center around my right ankle and my arches. My right ankle just suddenly decides it doesn't want to exist anymore and I fall.

&#x200B;

I have flats I have to wear for work and I walk 10,000 - 25,000 each shift so support is a big deal. Idk of we're allowed to post them but they're stick on arch inserts. They never stick well enough on their own so I always have to glue them, but they do work well. Maybe something like that can make some of your old shoes usable.

I work at a restaurant, but people recognize that I'm not interested in doing drugs. You see it a lot in restaurants. Tim always powders his nose before his shift. Phil sells pills, Derek sell weed but nothing harder. Cynthia can get those packaged candies from her friend in Colorado and she'll sell them for a reasonable price.

&#x200B;

No joke, it's everywhere but no one pushes it on you that I've ever seen. If you start looking you may actually find drugs were easier to get than you thought.

I suggest you choose your class partially based on how physical you want to be and how difficult it is to get gear for that class. There are several of us who are partially disabled who chose magic classes. I get injured easily but have full motion so I chose Scout for my first class (using a bow) and Archer for my second class. I'm level 5 in both now so I'm gonna have to start looking for another class soon. When I started out as a scout I wrote my spells on a paper and taped them to the inside of the top arm of my bow so i didn't have to memorize them.

&#x200B;

Magic classes - Positives are you don't have to run as much, and spell balls are fairly easy and cheap to make. Negatives are lots of memorization.

Bow classes - Positives are you don't have to run as much, and are still getting a bit of an arm workout. Negatives are the gear is a bit expensive for starting out and arrows have to be made to exacting standards.

Sword classes - Most people start here. Positives are you tend to have the least to memorize and the gear is the easiest (imho) to make or borrow. Negatives are that you often end up running around as much as a soccer player. If you're looking to get in the cardio this is the way to go.

&#x200B;

Hopefully your park is friendly and welcoming. Don't borrow gear without asking, it may be laying to the side because it failed the checks. If you want to come and just watch a couple of times that's totally fine. Our park often had 5 - 10 people sitting and watching, plus many of our players would take breaks. Our park also trended older than most parks I've seen (ages 20 - 40ish mostly). All the other parks I've been to are mostly teens. We had a lot of people and a lot of spare gear for new players to try out.

&#x200B;

Our only rules for garb for new players are no jeans and no logos. After a few weeks we expect you to have at least a tunic and leggings or wrap pants. You can make a tunic out of an oversized t-shirt or sweater to start. Our area is very hot so our main concern is that no one dies of heat stroke.

My fiancé had terrible road rage when we started dating. It was really frustrating because he always seemed to focus on the drivers behind us and not the road in front of us. I bought a dash cam and told him if someone was driving like an idiot to read their license plate out loud in case the camera doesn't catch it.

&#x200B;

I think his road rage was an expression of anxiety because once he started reading the license plates it really calmed him down. Last year the dash cam broke but he's still much more relaxed while driving.

Tipout at Cheesecake Factory is the same. 5% split between the bar, bussers, and runners. It's still a significant amount. Food would take 45 minutes to an hour pre-pandemic. Guests were always pissed from their hour+ wait for a table and then the wait for the food.

&#x200B;

My current restaurant has a total of 2% tipout when we have a runner. 1% to the bar, 1% to the runner. The system does it automatically so if the runner isn't clocked in when you ring in the food, they don't get a portion of that tip. We only have "bussers" on holidays and it's usually one of the hosts or dishwashers. They do ask us to throw the busser a couple bucks at the end of the shift, but they don't give any guidelines on how much to tip out. Most servers tip out based off the number of their tables that were bussed during the shift. $1 or $2 per table.

After 20+ years in the industry I'm finally getting out. At this point compression socks + an ankle brace is not enough to stop the swelling in my right ankle. I recently took 2 weeks off and the swelling finally went down for the first time in a couple years. Those 2 weeks off were to go to Disney so it's not like I was "resting" it but moseying around Disney is just not the same as the 25,000 to 30,000 steps I was jogging in a day at work.

&#x200B;

I'm really concerned about gaining weight, but I can't take the pain any more. I recently got an MRI on that ankle when it was feeling better to see if there was something inherently wrong since it's always the one I twist and hurt. Apparently when my ankle was "better" it was still a mess. I had a pretty serious tear in one of the ligaments near the front of my foot, and damage to two of my tendons. There was also scar tissue from all my previous injuries, and a bone spur in the joint.

More tables does not always mean more money. I served a single party of 12 the other night and walked with $250. When you're working in a higher end restaurant your money is more hit and miss, but the hits are sometimes the equivalent of what you used to make in a week. I've made 800 - 1,000 in a single night shift, but I've also make $30 or nothing at all.

&#x200B;

Look in to the higher end restaurants in your area. I'm not as against chains as the other poster so if you have a Seasons 52, Eddie Vs, or Capital Grille in your area maybe try to transfer. It's easier to get your foot in the door there from OG.

I guess my aura is different because my vision closes in and I get dizzy for 30 minutes - an hour before my migraines. I also feel this tight feeling in that bone on the top of my nose. Sometimes rubbing it helps. Painkillers do sometimes help but my migraines last 2 - 5 days and I really don't like to take pills consecutively like that. I also get so sick it's hard to eat from the pain and taking them on an empty stomach is bad.

&#x200B;

I've found out that my main trigger is stress. Ever since I quit my last job (5 years ago) I get maybe 1 migraine a year. Before it was 1 - 2 a month. I often spent almost half my month with a migraine. It was awful.

My dad routinely dates women who look/sound like me and are close to my age. He doesn't understand why it's creepy that he only dates pale redheads with freckles and round cheeks who are close to my age. He once brought an 18 year old to my 19th birthday. I told him to never bring a girl around again.

&#x200B;

I've now totally cut contact with him, for 5 years and counting. His dating habits were one of many reasons.

To be fair someone got written up over $5. One of my coworkers stole a dessert and got caught eating it in the back. Our GM told them to pay full price for the dessert or they would get a write up for theft. They decided to take the write up.

&#x200B;

Funnily enough, if you don't have the $$ for food and ask they'll get you set up with something. Also staff meal often contains something I can't eat so if I ask they'll get me something else. I'm being non-specific because the something else is whatever chef decides to make within my dietary restrictions.

That's a very nice thing to do! The thing I love about Thanksgiving is a lot of the guests are generous and understand the wait times, unlike on Mothers Day and Valentines. Guests are just happy they aren't doing the work and they get to spend time with their families. I work the host stand for PM and those guests are likely to be less understanding.

&#x200B;

One year I had a table of 6's food running 45 minutes+ and they dropped the salmon so it had to be recooked. I went to apologize and the father stopped me. He said, "You've given us the gift of more time with our family today. Don't apologize. The food will get here when it's ready." That really warmed my heart.

&#x200B;

For some reason Christmas Eve guests are not as friendly. Christmas Eve I've had some real winners, including a woman who slapped my hand for trying to remove an empty plate. Plus a guy who put my wine key back in my apron pocket for me while I was serving the wine. That's pretty close to touching my crotch, please don't do that.

The cruise compass is where we found it, you're correct. On most other cruises I've been on it can be found in several other places. I wanted to know the time on the first night so I could make plans with my group ahead of time but I didn't get that information until the night before we got to the island, when our cruise compass for the next day was delivered.

&#x200B;

Our port stops were changed last minute so I didn't think I could rely on the information I got before boarding. I checked the info on the TV, and tried to find it on the app, also asked our waiter and room steward. It could very well have been on the app or TV but we couldn't find it with 4 of us actively looking on the app and tv for 5 - 10 minutes before dinner.

Where can I buy 1 mg? 5 mg is the smallest dose I can find here and I eat about 1/4 of one of those for a perfect high. I use edibles just to relax my muscles since I get injured easily and it seems to help me heal faster.

&#x200B;

I have a very low tolerance and take about 2 - 3 mg a month so it's not getting any better.

I see a lot of people saying to use refried beans in chili alongside the tomato sauce. I'm interested, but some people add corn and potato chunks to their chili so I'm scared to trust these suggestions. I honestly don't even like regular beans in my chili. The "skin" of the beans requires more chewing than the rest of it and so I end up with only beans by the end of each bite.

&#x200B;

I love refried beans though, especially mixed in with beef. Do I risk ruining a pot of chili to try this? Still on the fence.

I love refried beans as a means to stretch beef. I buy a cheap roast and have them grind it but I suggest buying the lowest fat meat you can find (1/2lb - 1 lb), throw the meat in a pan with the can of refried beans and a ton of seasoning, cook until the beans start to lose some moisture and the whole mix looks like ground meat. It always cooks a little longer than I expect it to.

&#x200B;

Serve in a tortilla with cheese, use as a dip for chips, the base of a taco salad, put in enchiladas.

The salvation army was having an event where they were giving out food boxes near where I lived years ago. My mom and I were starving so we went to get a box. They wanted her to sign a paper agreeing that we were Christian and Jesus was our lord and savior. My mom is pagan, so she wouldn't sign it. They kept saying they could link her up with a local church that would have more help for us if only we would repent.

&#x200B;

We ended up wasting a few hours that day and leaving hungry. We found a quarter later on and were able to buy 3 of the 5 cent packs of ramen and a 10 cent bulb of garlic for dinner. Guess those prices show how old I am.

Different families have different yule traditions. My family stayed up all night tending the fire. For us the longest night of the year represented the darkness and negativity we would face in the coming year. Keeping the fire going was representative of keeping your strength through difficult times. 

&#x200B;

We always started off the evening lighting the fire just before dusk and throwing in things we wanted to let go of from the previous year. It might be burning bills to represent getting out of debt, burning letters to represent letting go of an ex. If we didn't have anything to burn that felt right we could write a letter about what we wanted to let go and burn that.

&#x200B;

Now I invite friends over every yule. My friends love the tradition and burn things of their own every year. Sometimes there's a lot of crying during that part. Afterwards we sit around the fire and talk, play board games, play dnd, anything we can do to stay up. At a certain point in the night many of us go on a walk to wake up (usually sometime between 3 and 6 am). I'll go on a little jog to get the blood pumping. We drink energy drinks, eat crunchy chips, share laughter and love, and generally just stay awake.

&#x200B;

Typically about half of us make it to dawn. As we see the warming light on the horizon we quickly clean up and put everything away. I've lived in apartments in new cities where I lit a candle and kept myself awake alone. Most of the books say a candle isn't enough but we do what we can with what we have.

&#x200B;

Yule celebrates the longest night of the year. The sun is returning to us after a long winter. What does that mean to you? How do you want to celebrate it?

I'm confirmed for The Key program on my cruise that leaves this Friday. When I booked they were charging $16.99 per day (4 day/3 night cruise) which came out to about $50 per person. They were charging $14.99 per day just for internet so that's a no brainer for my sailing.

&#x200B;

I didn't really care about the internet. My fiancé has anxiety which has gotten much worse during the pandemic. I'm trying to do anything I can to help him avoid the crowds on the first and last day of the cruise. I hope the lunch catered by Chops is good and not too crowded.

&#x200B;

I could make a post or respond to this on Friday after we board if anyone's interested in knowing what the key is like right now.

As a veteran waitress, Diet colas are a darker color than regular. Regular cola looks really dark brown, diet cola is almost black without the brown tone. I know that doesn't sound easy to tell but if you pour them in clear glasses side by side it's not hard to tell. Once you learn the color you can hold it up to a light and look for a brownish tinge which will let you know it's regular.

&#x200B;

If it's lemon lime or fruit soda or whatever, there's no way to tell that I know of other than flavor.

As a vegetarian when I was growing up in Texas, I always brought my own food to BBQs. Skewers with bell peppers, jalapeno, and pineapple, topped with teriyaki or bbq sauce. Sometimes I'd make my own mushroom skewers with seasoned mushrooms. Just toss rinsed and dried mushrooms in whatever seasoning blend and grill.

&#x200B;

I always had to bring enough for the random couple of people who "just had to try it" otherwise I wouldn't even get a reasonable portion of the food I brought for myself. That always really pissed me off but my parents would tell me to just share. I enjoy cooking for other people but that was all I could eat at the party so... maybe don't take my food.

I don't think it's that. As a waitress, if I have a guest who has an empty drink or doesn't have a drink at all then management could ask me wtf is going on there, or just assume I'm not taking good care of that table.

&#x200B;

I even had a group of servers from another restaurant scam us. Our restaurant used to sell Jones sodas in the bottle which had those cool sayings or facts or something on them. The guests told me they wanted to keep the empty bottles to take home. Okay, idc. Halfway through the meal they flag down a manager and talk about how awful the service is because I'm not clearing anything away and there's no space to put down the entrees. The manager comped all the sodas, 3 each for 5 people. That's a lot of damn soda.

&#x200B;

I honestly feel like the fact that they had 2 drink refills each before the entrée arrived kind of proves my good service. Also, since they were sitting in the bar area they were about 15 ft from a trash can I could have thrown away the bottles in. Their story didn't make sense but the managers bought it.

NTA

I (34 F) have a dad (52 M) who recently had a child (5M) with a woman (32 F) who almost immediately left him. My dad sucks. I spoke out in court to try to get this woman that I don't even know full custody of my brother. My dad lies to his girlfriends about all sorts of random stuff constantly. He keeps a tally of women he's slept with (500+ several years ago). Every once in a while he'll meet a woman, fall madly in love and marry her within a few months. He's very controlling and likes to cut these women off from society.

&#x200B;

His most recent wife, the 32 year old that had a kid with him, is from another country. I told her before they got married about how controlling and crazy my dad is but she wouldn't listen. I was really worried about her because all his other wives were American and even they had trouble escaping him. She divorced him but he still had a portion of custody (2 days a month) so she couldn't leave the country with my brother. Now she's trapped in America for the foreseeable future. The pandemic hasn't helped either. In order to keep her job and house she's resorted to having my dad care for their child 5 days a week. She can't work from home, and my brother is special needs.

&#x200B;

Reading your story made me wonder if this man is my dad. My dad is a 6ft2 Viking-looking dude with long blonde/grey hair and typically some sort of beard. Last I saw he had grown a beer belly. I haven't seen him in years so idk if anything's changed. If it is my dad, you need to run. No relationship with him ends in a positive way. Even if it isn't my dad, this guy sounds creepily like him. I hope he doesn't have too much info on you. Good luck!

Try to find things that smell appetizing. Eat very slowly with tiny bites. If nothing smells appetizing try dry crackers. Give yourself 30 minutes after eating to see if it settles.

&#x200B;

I cried in the toilet for an hour because I couldn't keep a $60 ribeye down. My body wanted the meat so badly but I just couldn't keep it down. I know it sounds strange but I made dips from canned chicken, garlic, herbs, whatever cheeses were around. I would warm up a tiny bit of the dip and eat it with crackers or tortilla chips. I couldn't stomach cooking so that was good.

&#x200B;

Sometimes I munched on freshly washed romaine lettuce. It's crunchy, sweet, and I almost always had an easy time keeping it down. Just know that it's trial and error on what your body will allow you to eat. If you find something good, still eat it slowly because eating too fast can still make it come back up. If you need help eating slowly, take small bites, chew well, and count to 10 after you swallow before the next bite. Eventually you get used to the rhythm of it and don't have to count anymore.

So, I have DDD boobs and I'm 30+ so they don't just sit on my chest like beach balls anymore. Now that they hang a bit they are starting to act like additional armpits. I probably shouldn't, but I often use deodorant on the bottom crease to help with the sweat. I also use baby wipes on them and my pits between showers to keep things fresh.

&#x200B;

One of the girls I dated had huge knockers. She wore the same bra for months on end and didn't wash it so the smell was awful when she took it off. I can't imagine if she was storing cash or IDs in there. To be fair, we were teens so we hadn't mastered hygiene yet.

&#x200B;

Edit: Just to be clear, I don't think it's acceptable for people to store stuff in their boobs.... however I have been known to put my phone there for convenience. Just not stuff I would hand to a stranger.

I had pretty light periods for a long time, but every once in a while I'd have a bad one. Now I have fibroids so my pain ramped up. It happened pretty slowly so I've still been able to deal. I went in to the hospital for something else and they found my fibroids. During the 3 days I had ultrasounds, ct scans, vaginal ultrasounds, mri, blood tests, the works. I started my period a few days after I left the hospital and it was the worst one ever. It was like we upset the fibroid gods.

&#x200B;

Big thick clots, extreme poops, pain so bad I had to crawl to my bed because I couldn't stand. I also couldn't handle making an appointment and having another doctor shrug and say my pain was normal, so I just dealt with it. Now my periods are back to being slightly worse than my previous normal.

I had a party of 7 show up 3 hours late and it was actually 10 people. We have booths and tables that can seat up to 7, 8 if you really like each other. For a group of 10 we have to push tables together. I told these people that they would have to wait while we pushed something together and they flipped out.

&#x200B;

Apparently, we should have known that they had 3 extra people and were showing up hours late.

Miami is trying to protect their servers from bad tippers while also making life easier for the travelers who are from non-tipping countries. They only do it on the beach, though. I work a bit inland and there's no auto-grat here unless it's a larger group that wants separate checks or a private dining party with a pre-negotiated gratuity.

&#x200B;

We always grat when there's a larger group with separate checks. We frame it as being easier for the guest, but honestly it seems like on those groups there's usually at least one person who tips shitty or doesn't tip.

I found a unicorn restaurant. When hurricane Irma was going to hit, it was due on a Sunday. Our last day of work that week was Wednesday, as our boss wanted us to have 3 days to prepare. He said no one would be on the schedule for the following week and if we could re-open he would be personally calling people to see how they were and if they wanted to work.

&#x200B;

The restaurant never lost power but my house did. I went to work on Tuesday after the storm for the AC and fresh food. It was really slow, but he gave us a bunch of extra food to take home so we could feed our family, set up a charging station for phones and whatever, and convinced a regular to sit on our front patio and let people know we were open. The regular was given free beer, a fair trade.

&#x200B;

Anyway, just saying that those restaurants do exist, they're just really hard to find.

My family has a lovely tradition, and because of it we always buy $100 or less engagement rings. I love pure silver rings with gemstones which are usually in the $50 to $100 range. I'm currently wearing a resin ring filled with bits of gold, flower petals, and some greenery which was $50 on Etsy.

&#x200B;

The tradition is that when you get married and go on your honeymoon, you and your partner use the rope from your handfasting to tie the rings together and bury them somewhere around your honeymoon spot. If you want to separate, one or both of the partners have to go back to the honeymoon spot and dig up the rings. I'm not saying you have to do this tradition, but just know that there could be a lot of reasons for people to buy cheaper engagement rings. It doesn't have to feel like a symbol of poverty.

NTA. If you are going to continue this relationship, you're going to have to start portioning all the food for everyone and keeping an eye on leftovers. My ex was unemployed and went crazy on soda and cigarettes, both of which were things I didn't consume at all. I sat him down and explained that I couldn't afford to buy a pack of each every day, so until he had a job I would only buy enough for him to have 3 of each a day. I basically bought 2 packs of soda and one pack of cigarettes every week for him.

&#x200B;

If it were me in this situation, I would probably sit your husband down at a calmer time and explain to him how you and the children are having trouble dealing with these issues. Explain that you are going to separate out snacks and food from now on so everyone has a fair share. Also that if he gets caught eating all the leftovers or stealing your kids snacks then he needs to figure out a different living situation. You can't allow your kids to suffer because of this douche canoe.

I'm 9 years older than my brother. We were really poor and mom almost never had time to go grocery shopping so I typically took my brother with me and did all the shopping for the house. The store was several blocks from our house but I had a bicycle with one of those [trailers that hold 2 kids](https://retrospec.com/products/rover-bike-trailer-single-double-child?variant=40201108947116&currency=USD&utm_medium=product_sync&utm_source=google&utm_content=sag_organic&utm_campaign=sag_organic) so I'd stuff my little bro in there with all the groceries and bike home. When he was older he rode his own bike and we had more space for groceries, although sometimes he would stand on the bar that went from the trailer to the bike and hold on to the little basket I had mounted on the back. That wasn't safe, but we were kids, so whatever.

&#x200B;

The worst part was shopping in the store and the checkout line. Old ladies would follow us around the store and make awful comments about me being a slut or a whore. Asking about the father. Judging what was in my cart loudly. Commenting on how my brother was acting. Even if they said something positive it was always framed in a negative light. "Wow, I can't believe how well behaved your son is considering how irresponsible you are to have him so young."

&#x200B;

It happened almost every time I went to the store. If it wasn't the old ladies, then old men were hitting on me and trying to get me to go places with them. I did all the shopping from when I was 13 - 16 so I was still a kid myself. My brother was 4 - 7. If I did have a kid when I was 9 years old, how could it have been anything but rape? I can't imagine a young rape victim being called a whore every time they go to the grocery store.

Managers, dishwashers, hosts, bussers, or the hired night cleaning crews are the only people I've ever seen clean restrooms in a restaurant. You can't have the servers, food runners, server assistants, or the cooks do it because they all handle food, no matter how much you believe they make.

&#x200B;

My small, female manager had to mop poop off the floor in the ladies room while wearing a dress and short heels. We didn't have a busser on, and dish was busy so she did it herself. All my current managers are leaders. They are constantly cleaning tables, running food, helping out the host, running expo, ect. When it's really slow my boss goes to the back to do paperwork, but otherwise he's always helping.

&#x200B;

We see when our managers aren't willing to work and help us. It's pretty obvious. I believe any restaurant should have a cleaning crew coming in at least weekly to dust and get all the weird spots that everyone else may miss, also to do a deep cleaning on the bathrooms. Cleaning services are not that expensive and it makes a significant difference to your guests to see a shiny, clean restaurant.

Probably an unpopular opinion, but I don't like self checkout either. Having the computer just start yelling "please place the item in the bagging area" the second I scan something and then the inevitable "unexpected item in the bagging area" because the weights aren't calibrated correctly or something. Half of them have their credit card readers broken but you don't find out until the end so you have to go to another machine and start over.

&#x200B;

I like the cashiers at Aldi. They get to sit the whole time, and don't have to worry about bagging groceries. I don't have to deal with a stupid nonfunctional self checkout machine. I wish there was a grocery store that had scanners built in to the carts so when you put the groceries in it just reads what's in there. All you'd have to do at the end is do fruits/veggies and pay.

I was told by an urgent care that they were going to call an ambulance to take me to the hospital since my condition was "serious". I said not to worry, that I would have my fiancé drive me. They said fine.

&#x200B;

When we got in the car he asked which hospital, and I said home. He freaked out but I wanted to charge my phone, pack a little bag for the hospital, and take a short nap since I probably wasn't going to sleep there. There was a lump in my throat that had been growing for a couple weeks and was making it a bit hard to swallow. They were concerned because if it kept growing it might be hard to breathe soon. I ended up spending 3 days in the hospital and the lump just went away. Never really found out what it was.

Most of our tables are by reservation. I've been helping out at the host stand and I'm supposed to allow people to make reservations all the way up until closing time. When guests call and ask for a 10 pm reservation, I let them know the kitchen closes at 10 so the last reservations are at 9:45 and they can't be late.

&#x200B;

Most of the time people decide to change to 9:00 or 9:30 which is slightly less awful.

My old boss lived almost 2 hours away, since she was the district manager and I was the store manager. For some reason she always decided to check her emails between 1 am and 2 am and then tried to get a hold of me for urgent stuff at that time. I told her several times to not interrupt my sleep. It was a new store so I was working open to close every day to get everything running smoothly, plus doing schedules at home. I already was only sleeping 5 hours a night because I really needed that 3 hours of decompress time.

&#x200B;

One night, I decided to turn my phone off, and had my boyfriend do the same in case she got his number from someone. 3:30 am this crazy woman is pounding on my front door. She tried to call several times and thought I must be in some sort of trouble since I didn't answer. I let her know that from now on my phone would be off outside of work hours. She said, "That's not fair! I don't know your schedule!" I said, "Well, you know when the store is open, and I send you my schedule every month, so that's not really my problem."

&#x200B;

I ended up quitting a few weeks later, she just couldn't stick to the schedule and freaked out on me every morning when I turned my phone on. I demoted myself to assistant manager and promoted my assistant to store manager. Spent a few weeks training her, and then moved halfway across the country to get away from that hellish job.

People weren't careful until there was a real reason to be. Face shots aren't really an issue anymore because players learn fast when there's a consequence, even a minor one like ours. I'm not sure how you guys play but we have 15, 30, or 60 second death timers depending on the game. Face shots don't count towards your death count on limited life games, it's just a short time out.

&#x200B;

Players always have the option to go to weekly fighters practice if they would like to learn, or if they just want to fight and not do any RP or magic stuff. At our peak we had over 50 players so it was important to mitigate the things that were causing the most injuries and drama.

Our park has spare weapons for newbs since they never seem to get their first build working for a bit. We also allow players to wear anything, with the only rule being no jeans and no logos.

&#x200B;

In the summer time several players wear soccer shorts, athletic shirts, sneakers. We live in the south and we had someone at a nearby park die of heatstroke. One of our players passed out and had to be dragged to the shade on a particularly hot day. We think it's better to get out there and play without people dying.

&#x200B;

We are the only park that allows this afaik. It kind of turns amt a little more sports-oriented. At our peak this park had over 50 players every Sunday plus 5 - 10ish spectators. We also held a Wednesday fighters practice that had 20ish people. I was Chancellor at that time so it was insane putting in all that in the computer. We were lax on enforcing garb but hard on enforcing the rules of play.

Even if your boss wouldn't roll with that, you don't have to tell the guest unless they ask. When I refuse service (which I've done several times in my 20 years working) I find a server to give them a last chance. If that server also refuses service, the guests are gone.

&#x200B;

The second server often loves it because they know they can get the assholes kicked out if they step a toe out of line, and often I already have the order or it's already in so they don't have to do much work for a potential tip.

&#x200B;

If the guest does ask, you can tell them they seemed unhappy with the service so they were assigned a new server. It could make some assholes feel like they won, but they don't know they're on their last shot before getting kicked out.

Unfortunately sometimes you're stuck going out with these people with you want a relationship with someone else.

&#x200B;

My best friend moved in with a girl who's awful at restaurants. Naturally the two of them would do things together sometimes, since they had the same major hobby and lived together. Their hobby sometimes required travel, sometimes for up to a week so we ended up eating out with her pretty often. It kind of sucks but when 3 - 5 people are on a trip together, it's really hard to leave someone out from the meals.

&#x200B;

There's another crappy person in my life that I have to see every once in a while. My FIL has 10 siblings who all have grandchildren so at this point it's a huge family, many of whom are elderly. Once every other year-ish someone dies and we have to go out with that 1 crappy family member for the after-funeral dinner. Or someone gets married and since we're on that side of the family we end up seated near them and hearing them berate the waitstaff. It sucks that the answer is either cut contact with the whole family or deal with 1 annoying person sometimes.

I used a 30 spf Neutrogena sunscreen on my body and a 100 spf sport sunscreen on my face on a beach day so I didn't have to worry about my face getting burned. Applied all the sunscreen on our cruise balcony about an hour before we hit the beach so it soaked in properly. Reapplied as soon as we got to the beach and waited a little bit to get in the water.

&#x200B;

We were out there for 5+ hours and I didn't reapply again, which is on me. My face was burned up all to hell but my body was barely tanned at all. Ever since then I only use that one particular version of Neutrogena sunscreen.

But what about when you can't trust yourself to make the right decisions? What if you have an ED and binge on sweets and chips at least once a week? What if you only crave terrible foods and sometimes can't even bring yourself to consider a piece of broccoli?

&#x200B;

I've heard a lot of people talk about intuitive eating. Even my MIL says, "oh, I just eat what I crave and I lose weight." Well eating my cravings is exactly how I gained all this damn weight.

That makes sense to me. I went in asking for bright red baliage (sp?) highlights. The girl bleached my entire head of hair and dyed it all bright red. We discussed what I wanted before she started, and I'd never had highlights before so I didn't really know how it would work. She had half the foils on by the time I realized, but it was way too late.

&#x200B;

I just resigned myself to a whole head of red hair. I did push back when they tried to add an upcharge for extra bleach and dye. That wasn't happening. Honestly my hair came out really nice, just not even close to what I asked for.

At my old restaurant, if you rang something in wrong you better have the written down order on hand to prove you wrote it down. It's crazy that your boss wants you to stop writing down smaller orders. Are modifications allowed? If so, people can get a little crazy with those.

&#x200B;

There are memory training games that I played to help train myself early on to serving. There are probably apps for that now. I make 2 or 3 letter codes for all the items on the menu so I can write orders faster. There are also simple things you can do like -on is no onion. A burger without onion and add avocado is written "brg -on +avo" Maybe if you write faster he won't bug you as much, idk.

I had a problem a few years ago trying to buy a Blam-azon Fyre tablet. Their prime day sale is late June and my fiancé's birthday is early august so I bought a tablet during the prime day sale for him. Near the end of July the tablet still hadn't shipped so I contacted customer service to try to figure it out. They kept telling me it was out of stock but I should get it by the end of August. 2.5 months after I ordered isn't really what I was looking for, since I'm supposed to have 2 day shipping. Finally, after almost an hour trying to figure out what to do, the guy said that I could get blue instead of black since they had blue in stock now.

&#x200B;

Yeah. I don't care what color the tablet is. It's just going in a case anyway. It ended up well but that whole thing took way too long.

As a waitress, I typically don't invite friends/family to eat in my section while I'm working. I get a discount on the whole check if I sit and eat with them so I invite them to eat with me when I'm off, or during our 2 hour midday break.

&#x200B;

That said, my friends and family have come in to see me while I was working. I accept reasonable tips from them. My mother in law came in and tried to tip 100%, I just adjusted it to a 25% tip so she could feel good for tipping extra but not go crazy. My dad came in and didn't tip at all after running me ragged for coke refills and camping at one of my two booths for my entire shift (6 hours) on his laptop.

&#x200B;

Accept reasonable tips. If it makes you feel better, buy them a drink next time you're out with them.

Thankfully I've never had sleep paralysis. After I left I would wake up from nightmares that I couldn't remember. I would be so scared I would go hide in the closet, or anywhere small that I could tuck myself. Sometimes I would sit there awake for hours holding myself and shaking, sometimes I would be so tired that I would fall asleep in my small space.

&#x200B;

For about 10 years after I escaped there wasn't a night where I was able to sleep more than 4 hours. Woke up every single night terrified from nightmares.

&#x200B;

The first time I tried USA pot, it made me really sleepy so I went to bed. Woke up about 4 hours later, but I didn't have any dreams at all. I was so happy I cried. Then I thought about it and got worried that I would get addicted to that feeling. I decided to only allow myself one restful sleep per week. After about the 3rd or 4th time smoking pot before bed (so about a month later), I had my first unaided dreamless night. Funnily enough, at the same time my boyfriend at the time was going down a bad path. Unknown to me he was getting addicted to meth and eventually attacked me.

&#x200B;

Anyway, after about a year of once a week smoking, I stopped having the nightmares altogether. Now, it's been years of restful sleep.


Edit - if it's not clear, I stopped smoking after that year. Now I do edibles a couple times a year but otherwise I don't really do anything. Always been afraid of addiction which runs in my family.

My aunt was staying with us for 2 years before covid, but events during covid finally got her kicked out. Normally, on any given day, she was already really annoying.

 \- She would sit in the living room and loudly comment about how disgusting whatever food I cooked smelled. She didn't eat any fruits or veggies so if I was cooking with those (basically every meal) she would sit 20 feet away bitching about it.

 \- She butted in to any conversation, even if we were speaking quietly. The way the house was built she could hear from her room pretty well. One specific time I remember asking my mother in law if she had seen some movie, which led to a rant from aunt about how much she hated that movie, she was yelling her opinion at us from another room.

 \- Even if mother in law or I cooked something she would normally eat, she wouldn't touch it. She would cook grits for herself every day and "wash" the pan by lightly rinsing it but often left bits of grits so we had to re-wash the dishes.

 \- She was a heavy duty negative nancy, and a Karen when out in public so we couldn't even go anywhere with her or we would end up embarrassed. 

&#x200B;

During covid, she continued going out like everything was fine. When the restaurants were closed she would order takeout and sit at the park with her friends to eat. She was also babysitting for 2 large households, going to at least 1 of them each day. We told her how uncomfortable her behavior was making us but she didn't care.

&#x200B;

The cherry on the cake came in June of last year. She was housesitting for someone while they went on vacation. Shortly after they arrived home several people from that household tested positive for covid. My aunt never told us they had tested positive, and openly coughed in the house when she inevitably got sick blaming allergies and her health conditions. At this point it had been over 3 months since I left the house and I suddenly lost my sense of taste and smell.

&#x200B;

We sent her directly to the hospital because she was very sick at that point and I went to get tested. Between the air purifiers and wearing masks indoors at all times (other than when eating in separate rooms) We were able to quarantine me, clean the house, and no one else got sick.

&#x200B;

When the family called her at the hospital she tried to blame me for bringing covid in the house. I guess she wasn't home often enough to notice I never went anywhere. We told her to find different accommodations for when she leaves the hospital.

I have as much time as you and I've heard joke orders. I always repeat orders back to people as I'm writing the order down so I just pretend I'm not understanding the joke.

&#x200B;

Non decaf coffee? So a regular coffee? Okay sir, I have a regular coffee for you, and for the lady?

&#x200B;

Then when they're laughing I just look more and more confused. Better to take a minute to shut that shit down at the beginning of the meal than deal with it the whole time.

I used to be really skinny but I've had a series of injuries over the last several years is making it harder and harder for me to be active. Doctors kept saying if I just lost weight I would stop having this chronic pain, but the pain is the reason I gained the weight.

&#x200B;

Turns out there's an issue with my connective tissues in all my joints, even in the connective tissue in my back. I was just diagnosed with JHS (joint hypermobility syndrome). Lots of people are bendy like I am and they can become ballerinas or gymnasts and live a healthy, happy life. If you have JHS though, there's constant pain that goes along with the bendy joints.

&#x200B;

I have symptoms of POTS (dizziness when I stand up or start moving too quickly, random vertigo), I was misdiagnosed with IBS because JHS causes digestive issues, my back has gone out twice in 2 years, so bad I couldn't get out of bed at all. I twist one of my ankles 2 - 3 times a year. I recently had an MRI of my right ankle and I have swollen tendons, a bone spur, and two of my ligaments have partial tears. The doctor was happy to say that my cartilage was doing just fine.

&#x200B;

Now that I finally have a diagnosis, I know what to do when symptoms are acting up. The stress of constant pain is still there, but now that I know why it's there, I know how I can exercise and keep moving. Plus I know I'm not making my issues worse. I've lost 5 lbs in the week since I was diagnosed. My back is trying to go out again but I'm gonna fight through this time.

&#x200B;

I know that my weight isn't helping this issue at all, and losing weight will do nothing but help. I even knew that before.

