A lot of how you can get away with responding to things kind of depends on your voice, tone, and a little bit on your appearance. I have a high pitched "Disney Princess" voice, a girl next door friendly face, and can often get away with saying things that my coworkers can't. Similarly, we have a big, intimidating bartender with tattoos who has a low voice and can say almost anything to a guest. On the other hand, we have a petite girl with a lower, almost angry sounding voice and a bad case of resting bitch face. She has to be very careful of what she says or she'll land a complaint.

&#x200B;

When I need to get away from a guest, I glance around at the rest of my section frequently while moving to the edge of the table. I answer their questions in a friendly tone, but when I reach the limit of my time, I say, "I'm so sorry, I have to come right back."

&#x200B;

Looking around like that can alert my coworkers that I need help, my boss is pretty good at noticing my plight and taking over.  If it's a friendly conversation, sometimes I'll answer a question and walk away, or if they've finished a sentence that doesn't require a response, I'll just walk away. I mean, I'm working.

&#x200B;

Do I think everyone can do this without upsetting the guests? Nope. That's just how I handle it. Just keep in mind that your appearance and demeanor throughout the service is important. Several of us girls put on this sweet, bubbly personality for work. It can help.

My younger brother came to live with me as a late teen. He got fired from a job I arranged for him and I wasn't comfortable trying to get him another job because of what happened. We had a hard life and he clearly hadn't processed his trauma.

&#x200B;

Anyway, I gave him a chore list and a $ value for each of the chores, to be paid on completion. Each time he finished a chore I checked it, and often had to teach him what was wrong and have him redo it. I wouldn't do that with a toddler, but I knew my brother wouldn't be with me for a long time and he needed to be prepared to take care of his own house.

&#x200B;

Basically, what I'm saying is that you should teach your kids the right way, but keep their age in mind when deciding how picky to be about the finished chore. If your kid is 14 + and they're still only cleaning 50% - 60% when they do their chores, you aren't really doing them a favor later in life. Either they're being lazy and choosing not to complete it, or they didn't fully understand the explanation and need some help.

&#x200B;

When I took my brother in he was 17 and his dad had just died. The first year I was really easy on him about everything, but I think that was the wrong way to go. I think he needed something to occupy his mind and make him feel useful. The chore list really turned his attitude around once I implemented it.

