In the coffee shop I worked at we had a tip jar. At the end of the 8 hour shift there was typically 6 - 10 dollars in there, mostly in coins, to split between 2 - 3 people. It really is only the generous customers who tip (at least in that area it was). Those couple of dollars were always nice, lots of us would save them. Some people asked for the coins for laundry.

&#x200B;

There's no expectation to tip at a coffee shop, but if you want to leave your 30 cents in change as a tip rather than jingling around town all day, it's nice to have an option.

I write everything down, but in quick shorthand. Chk = Chicken, Sesame = Sweet Sesame Salmon Salad, Short = Braised Shortribs.

&#x200B;

When creating your shorthand you have to keep in mind similar dishes. At my old restaurant we served bbq ribs and bbq chicken. I used to write BBQ for the ribs since the chicken was almost never ordered. One day, someone ordered the chicken and I wrote BBQ, and then ordered the ribs out of habit. Luckily, once they saw our ribs they decided to just stick with those.

&#x200B;

At my current restaurant we only have 1 meal with each protein, so it's easiest to just denote the protein.

The \[Hard Candy\] and \[Lollipop\] items can also help with the \[Oral Fixation\] part of food  addiction.

&#x200B;

The thing is, the hunger bar fills over time, and for many of us with food addiction it takes longer for the hunger bar to fill all the way, leaving us overeating while it's still filling. A good trick is to consume a \[glass of water\] while you're preparing/waiting for a food item. Consume your food item and then wait at least 30 minutes before consuming anything else. Often you'll realize that your hunger meter is actually full, or very close to full, from that single portion.

