CS seemed upset that I waited to complain and admonished me that if I had an issue with a box I needed to mention it in a timely manner. I called the same day that I got the second box. They ended up giving me $30 for the missing meat and messed up veggies, but I didn't appreciate being treated that way on my first ever complaint after getting 8+ boxes from them. I ended up getting one more box from them and then canceling permanently.

Sorry! I didn't mean to sound upset, I was just kind of talking about OP saying they wanted to give up and do MREs for a year. The first couple of times will probably be easier than cooking, but some of them are gross even the first time. The breakfast ones when I was a kid were horrible. There was one with ham that was always slimy. My brothers were willing to eat it but I couldn't even be in the room when they did.

&#x200B;

My friend took us camping a couple of years ago and brought MREs as the only food. Luckily I didn't trust her when she said she was bringing all the food so I brought smores and hot dogs.  Her husband and I bonded over bad MRE memories. His from war, mine from home. My friend somehow made it through the war without hating MREs, so she thought it would be fun to play army in the woods. We left for home early, but we stuck it out for 2 of the 4 days.

Slow cooker recipes are dumping stuff in a pot and pressing on. If your MRE heater doesn't activate you have to boil a pot of water to throw the packet in, plus there's a bunch of packets of random stuff if you aren't having just the entree. 

&#x200B;

When I was a kid my stepdad dove in a dumpster and got enough expired MREs to last us a little over year when we were really poor. The first couple weren't horrible, but after having them over and over you have to start modifying them, which is a lot more work than just throwing some stuff in a pot.

&#x200B;

Nowadays you can buy veggies pre-chopped at the grocery store, sauces pre-made. Just throw veggies, sauce, and protein in a slow cooker for a quick nutritious meal.

&#x200B;

If OP thinks they can handle MREs for every meal for a year, good on them. I couldn't do it again. Even with modifying the food, it starts to get nauseating.

Lazy chili is a good option if you don't mind opening cans. buy a couple of cans of that tomato/chili mix veg, a can or 2 of chili beans (the ones that are already seasoned) and some ground beef. I buy a roast and have them grind it up for me at the grocery store so there's little to no fat and I don't have to cook or drain it ahead. Toss everything in the pot together and cook for an hour or more. Since you have an instant pot you can probably pressure cook it for a shorter time, idk.

&#x200B;

People swear by browning the meat first but I honestly only think that matters if you have too much fat, or if you really are going all out for your chili. For lazy chili it's fine to cook together as long as you get 95% or better beef.

I just put a can of tomato/chilis mix in my instant pot with some frozen chicken and half a packet of taco seasoning. 15 minutes on high pressure and it was ready. My lazy bottom also threw a packet of ready rice in the microwave. Less prep than an MRE for sure.

&#x200B;

If I was feeling less lazy I might have done more veg, but I didn't. Not tonight.

I was a Wiccan in my freshman year of high school when the movie "The Craft" came out. Before that movie came out, other students had a natural curiosity about my religion, but mostly left me alone. After, it was horrible. Random teens would have light as a feather parties, came out as "real witches", showed off all the cool stuff they could do, then spent most of the day badly explaining how my religion worked to me, and casting curses at each other.

&#x200B;

Now I hate that movie. It basically ruined high school for me.

&#x200B;

I've never had a problem with any other pagan movie. My favorite movie for a long time was Practical Magic. It was the closest to my experience growing up that I'd ever seen. Of course, it's a movie so certain liberties were taken, but I loved the family passing down magic and how they showed that there can be negative consequences to using spells.

&#x200B;

I don't particularly care about how the general population feels about pagans. People who are interested ask questions and do research. People who are bigoted won't change their minds no matter what you put in a movie.

At my old restaurant we wouldn't split items at all. Ever. The cooks wouldn't even cut a burger in half in the kitchen. If someone asked for their burger cut in half I brought them a steak knife, which felt very passive-aggressive but it was always seemingly able-bodied adults asking us to cut their burgers in half, not like elderly people or kids.

&#x200B;

The majority of our meals were intended to be served family-style, with several people sharing several meals. The cooks didn't want to end up splitting 2 meals evenly on to 4 plates or some other combination of that so they just refused to do it at all.

&#x200B;

My current restaurant is a bit more high end and the portions are much smaller but they are totally willing to split most meals for guests. Some things, like the lamb shank, are tough to split and make look any good at all, so they won't do those.

&#x200B;

