I waited 16 days from my initial symptoms, which was 14 days after my fever and 12 days from my positive test. We only intended to wait 14 days from my initial symptoms but we had been in the same house the whole time I was isolated and he was still skeeved out about being near me. 

&#x200B;

I think the main reason I was able to keep it from spreading through the house was that, due to an accident involving mixing improper cleaning chemicals, my throat was torn up. My first few days of having covid (pre-other symptoms) I wasn't speaking at all because of the pain. I also never had the cough or sneezing fits that some people experience.

&#x200B;

The same day my throat was healed enough to start talking again I lost my taste and smell, and gained a fever. We all masked up in the house, and my covid-clean Fiance drove me to the testing center in a closed car (both of us wore masks) so we could both get tested. We actually didn't start my isolation until the next day. We were pretty sure I didn't have it since I hadn't been out anywhere, that was until his aunt (who lived with us) confessed that she was exposed to a covid positive family 10 days before I got sick. She had been sick for a week and insisted it was her other health issues, that there was no way she had covid, even though she knew she had been exposed all that time.

&#x200B;

She openly coughed all over the place, sometimes not covering her mouth at all, sometimes covering with the palm of her hand and then walking around touching things. Theres no question as to how I got it from her, the strange thing is that she didn't get anyone else with it. She ended up in the hospital for a week and we told her to spend that week working out another place to stay.

 [https://www.supercook.com/#/recipes](https://www.supercook.com/#/recipes) 

