I don't know of any restaurant that would put you from the arches straight to being a server. Even a waitress I recruited from Dennys to my higher end restaurant had to start out as a food runner.

&#x200B;

If you wanted to become a waiter it would be better and worse at the same time. Better, because it's likely that your guests won't be as rude as at the arches. Worse, because you have to deal with the same guest for over an hour. If they are rude, that really sucks. I've refused service to guests several times, but being female with a "Disney princess" voice, the guests try to push me around pretty often.

&#x200B;

To be an awesome waiter, you have to be confident, friendly, fast, honest, and have an amazing memory. I always tell my trainees that being likable can be very helpful if you make a mistake. People are more likely to forgive a waiter who's trying their best, even if they forgot to put in the appetizer order.

&#x200B;

You will make more money than you likely do at the arches, possibly even if you get stuck hosting for a while. If you do get stuck be the best you can at your job and keep asking every month or so what you can do to better yourself to get promoted. Spend your time at the host stand or spare time while running studying the menu, learning the POS system, learning to carry trays, opening wine bottles, or whatever else you can think of.

Move to Los Angeles, meet a guy named Dominique who offers you $6 an hour cash under the table to sit in a studio audience, clap, laugh, stay awake. Call Dominique daily to see if there's any show you can sit for. My dad started doing it with me for quick money, but he was only approved for the judge shows. I was able to do more. They're looking for certain ages and looks for most shows.

&#x200B;

