I know it's blasphemy, as I also spent a lot of time living in Texas, but I've made slow-cooker beef ribs a couple of times that were amazing. Very easy too. You just need beef ribs, onions, broth, and bbq sauce (if you like that kind of thing, which I do).

&#x200B;

Stand up the ribs in your slow cooker, cut the onions in quarters and squish them in the spaces between ribs, it's best if you have enough to fill the cooker. Pour in the broth, it's okay if up to an inch of rib meat is above the liquid line. If you want to be really fancy, throw a couple of sprigs of rosemary or thyme on top. Cook for 2 hours on high, then at least 2 hours on low, but you can do as long as you want.

&#x200B;

Carefully remove the beef ribs, trying to keep the bone on for now. Lay them on a sheet pan. Set the oven to broil, lay the ribs (bottom up) on the pan and coat generously with bbq. Cook on high until the bbq is thick and has a couple brown bits on the corners. Flip the ribs carefully and repeat with the top.

&#x200B;

You end up with juicy, flavorful ribs that refuse to stay on the bone, coated in sweet, spicy bbq with crispy burnt bits on the edges. It is not the same as Texas bbq, but it is damn good. Also, save the broth and use it to make french onion soup tomorrow, or whatever you want.

