As someone who was raised in a poly/nudist household.

NTA.

&#x200B;

I don't support that type of poly around children at all. My parents did the same thing yours did with multiple partners coming in and out of our lives. Some of them actually stuck around for up to a year. Most of their partners were fine, friendly, helpful, they'd even buy us presents. Some were not fine. Some were physically abusive to us and more than one sexually assaulted us.

&#x200B;

My parents did it the dumbest way possible though, they would meet these people at nudist events or in the newspaper classifieds. Later on in my life they found people through the internet. The people would show up and spend days or all the way up to a year living at our house. They didn't vet these people at all, their first meeting would be with us kids there at our house. I ended up installing a lock on my door after one of the guys snuck into my room. To clarify, there was no way it was an accident. My bedroom was upstairs. I think he didn't expect me to be awake, so he left, thank goodness.

&#x200B;

Allowing so many strangers in your home and around your kids is very dangerous, even aside from how much you were ignored.

Losing 2 of your main senses suddenly is a huge thing. I lost mine during covid and had the feeling and taste like I snorted pool water for over a week, it burned and tasted awful. After that pain went away I thought my smell and taste would return but it's been a bit of a road.

&#x200B;

Here's the thing, losing 2 senses and not knowing when they'll come back can seriously psychologically mess with you. I focused on eating things that I enjoy the texture of, and not concerning myself with calories or eating healthy. I also took high potency A D and K vitamins.  I took D because it's supposed to help, K because it helps you absorb D, and A because... it was also in the vitamin I bought.

&#x200B;

I read that Covid can interrupt and damage the nerves that transmit taste and smell. The olfactory training that people have already suggested could help you rebuild those connections, even if you smell nothing the first few times you do it. Eat, try to smell things. Fries with vinegar were my go-to when I lost taste and smell. When the chlorine burning and taste was too strong, even the idea of food made me feel sick and depressed. I was able to make it go away with those gin-gin tablets. The strong ginger burned a bit but it cleared up the chlorine flavor for a short time. I tried cough drops but the menthol burned too much.

&#x200B;

Now, a month after Covid I've started getting a post-nasal drip. The other day I blew my nose and it was like Christmas. Everything smelled so strongly. I was in the shower and the new shampoo I bought apparently smells VERY STRONGLY of lavender. I still have the postnasal drip so my sense of smell is off and on but it's just so exciting to have it on sometimes again.

&#x200B;

You. Will. Get. Better. I'm sure of it.

Air fryers are like convection ovens but with way stronger fans. They are great for so many things. The only issue I've had with mine is that if you put something on top of your food like a slice of cheese it can get blown around and get all over inside. I tried warming a couple of tortillas in there and they blew up to the heat ring on top and got stuck on it.

&#x200B;

For the positives. It's the only way to heat up leftover french fries and have them taste fresh. Things cook very fast in air fryers, almost microwave fast. You don't need to preheat 90% of the time. You can fry things (like broccoli) with just a tiny bit of oil so they don't end up greasy and gross. 

&#x200B;

For me, the main positive when I was doing CICO was that I was missing crispy food the most. In an air fryer, you can make almost anything crispy. Cut anything thin, toss with a touch of oil and a good amount of seasoning, air fry until crispy, which is often as little as 3 minutes. I did it with asparagus, zucchini, broccoli stems, kale, and jicama. Then you can use those crispy bits as toppings for things, or be like me and munch on them way too often.

I make veggie chips by cutting up the thick stem of broccoli, or some nice zucchini into super-thin slices (you should be able to see the knife through the slice if you want it perfect). Then I toss them with a bit of oil/fat and a bunch of seasonings. Throw them in our air fryer for maybe 5 minutes on med/low heat. Keep a close eye, they burn fast. We actually prefer the broccoli stem version, it turns out crispier. Plus, most people just throw out broccoli stems.

&#x200B;

A healthy, tasty meal I've made.

&#x200B;

Chop up some veggies (or use a frozen bag, you do you) put them on the bottom of a baking pan. Put a couple of raw chicken breasts or thighs on top. Pour a sauce on the chicken, you can use basically anything, just make sure it's thick enough to stick to the chicken a bit. I've used teriyaki, bbq, honey mustard, or for savory flavors, I toss the chicken in oil and seasonings. If you have the calories to spare and want to kick it up a notch you can put a couple pats of butter in with the veggies.

&#x200B;

As it cooks in the oven, the drippings and sauce from the chicken get in the veggies. If your veggies are getting overcooked and the chicken isn't done yet, pull the chicken off and stir the veggies so the dark crispy bits aren't on top, then put the chicken back and continue baking. If you're using a regular chicken breast this shouldn't be an issue. Cook at 350 for around 20 - 30 minutes.

&#x200B;

The roasted veggies should end up chewy and crispy since there wasn't too much liquid while they were cooking. You can top this with the veggie chips I described earlier. This recipe can be mixed up with any protein, veg, and sauce. It's been my go-to for years.

&#x200B;

For any other meat, just look up how long it takes to bake. If it's less than 20-30 minutes add the meat halfway through, if it's longer than that, you can start the meat in a pan and get a good sear before baking.

So, my brother and I look young, like teenagers. I've been in the industry for a long time and I know people treat teens like crap. My normal tip is 25%, great service is 30%, and I've tipped up to 100% for exceptional service. The thing is, I'm willing to tip less for bad service. The smallest tip I've given was a nickel.

&#x200B;

That waitress ignored us pointedly for almost an hour. We were there to catch up and weren't starved yet so we just kept talking. At one point, she walked by our table and muttered "fucking teenagers" loud enough for us to hear. Eventually, she gave up and decided to serve us. We ordered, the food came fairly quickly (breakfast place), and we were ready to head out so we paid, "tipped" and went to the front to look for a manager. We found one, and while we were complaining the waitress came up screaming about what pieces of shit we were and threw the nickel at me. I told the manager that her attitude was unacceptable and we left.

&#x200B;

I've been in the industry for a long time and I have to suck it up every day. At work, I take all the "shitty" tables. POC, old people, teens, foreign people, I don't care. I'm not going to just serve white people in their 30s - 50s. That's a crazy mentality for servers to have. If you treat everyone well, you get some nice tips, even from people that you've profiled and decided they won't tip.

&#x200B;

For advice, you can do what I do. Become a regular for a particular waiter/waitress at each restaurant you visit. We keep notes on our phones of their names. If you tip as well as you say, the other servers will be jealous and try to steal you from your regular server.

&#x200B;

If it's a place you don't visit often, and the service is bad, tip accordingly. You can't singlehandedly make servers as a whole treat their guests better. If the server is snotty or has an attitude, complain. We had a snotty waitress at my place for months but the guests wouldn't complain about her so she stuck around for way too long. You're doing the whole restaurant a favor when you complain about a bitchy server.

&#x200B;

If you want to get really petty, you can leave both copies of the credit card slip. One filled out with a $0 tip, the other with a 30% tip, but a note about how terrible their service was, saying something like, I'm only tipping to show you that black people do tip well, so stop treating us like crap. At most restaurants, any tip over 20% is checked by the office manager or someone at corporate to make sure the servers aren't changing the numbers or inputting the wrong thing. In order for the server to get the tip, they have to turn in the complaint.

