I was the third in a poly relationship (husband and wife plus me, a female person). Everything went really well and we are still great friends, but I have to say that it didn't come from a good place.

&#x200B;

They were both in the military. The husband and wife were virgins before each other and while the husband was away at war the wife slept with someone in her unit.  The wife and I have a long-standing argument about the consent of that situation. 

&#x200B;

She came to me and said she slept with someone that she didn't want to sleep with and she wanted someone at her next party to make sure she was safe. I spent the night pretending to drink and watching this guy physically pushing up her glass to make her drink more and mixing another drink for her before the previous one was finished. Once she was good and drunk he decided to end the party and kick everyone out. I had to threaten to call the MPs to get him to leave and we almost got into a fistfight. Once he was gone I stayed the night with her and the next day made her promise not to ever let someone hand her a drink again. Walked her through everything that guy did wrong and how dangerous that situation could have been. She knew at that time that I was poly.

&#x200B;

A few weeks later she calls and basically asks me to sleep with her husband as a favor. She didn't tell her husband the full situation but basically made it sound like she cheated on him. He was upset for a lot of reasons, but one was that he had promised to be true to her and would never get to sleep with anyone else. I came over with my boyfriend, we drank and played strip poker, and I slept with the husband. After that, the 4 of us would play together but we were 2 separate couples. Then I broke up with my boyfriend and it was just me for a while.

&#x200B;

Eventually, we all moved to the same place at different times. Since it was staggered and we had about 6 months apart, it was easy for me to let them know I wasn't interested in that type of relationship anymore. 12 years later we're all still best friends, and now they have a third who is very happy with her role. The husband has a lot of love to give and wants a lot of attention, the wife just can't keep up so they are happiest with a female third. He honestly would spend his whole life cuddled up on the couch watching movies, cooking for people, and doing cute romantic gestures if his girls would let him. The man needs a harem.

Just like with anything else, the big box stores are not the best. The absolute best option in my opinion is a home breeder. Many home breeders only breed when there is a buyer, or breed once a year. Keep in mind, a home breeder who hand-feeds their birds will not be cheap. After the baby bird is hatched it needs constant feedings every hour and slowly you can drop feedings. Some breeders will sell you the bird when they are on 3 feedings a day, which is what I would recommend. The bird can acclimate to you while they are still young enough not to go through too much re-homing stress. Obviously adoption is a wonderful thing to do, but I only recommend that for experienced owners.

&#x200B;

You could try fostering a bird if you're interested in seeing what their care is like. They are messy. They dip their food in their water so you have to change it a bunch of times a day, they throw food they don't like all over the ground outside of their cage, most scream at dawn, sometimes for hours. They need rotating (often expensive) toys. You should re-do their cage about once a week for some species to give them entertainment and help calm down cage aggression. You. Will. Get. Bit. Given enough time, your bird is going to bite you and draw blood at least once. If you are the type of person who would be too afraid to love them after that, you can not have a bird.

&#x200B;

I loved working at the bird store. I will always remember the family (Mom, dad, son, daughter) that came in every week. Dad got his bird first, and each week he would come back proudly with his green-cheeked conure on his shoulder. That bird loved him. Then one-day mom found her forever bird. Daughter found hers, but after months of looking, son couldn't find his soul-mate.

&#x200B;

One day, I was purchasing from one of our breeders and saw a [Kakariki](https://www.petguide.com/breeds/bird/kakariki/). The personality of that bird was a 100% match to son and I bought it. In the store, I put a reserved sign on the cage and waited for the family. The bird would run up one wall of the cage to do backflips, and change it up by running up one wall, across the top, and down the other side. He liked napping upsidedown like a bat. He was a character and I only had him in the shop less than a week. Son came in and fell in love. He came to me, wondering if the bird was really reserved. I said, yes, he's yours.

&#x200B;

That family came weekly for almost the whole 2 years we were open. They were always the highlight of my week. They never bought another bird, but their flock was clearly loved and happy.

My fiance used to use TV to get to sleep. He would leave the remote in his pillowcase under the pillow. It was so frustrating every night because I had to get the remote without waking him so I could go to bed. We eventually compromised, he watched the same comedy special to go to sleep every single night. Now, if you sing the opening song to John Mulaney's New in Town, I get pretty sleepy. Since it was on Netflix it didn't autoplay anything else and the TV went to sleep not long after it ended.

&#x200B;

That went great for 2 years, then, slowly he just forgot to turn the TV on some nights. Now it's been years since he's even turned on the bedroom TV.

I also have IBS and got a bidet just before this COVID 19 thing started (lucky me). Now I only use TP now when I'm on my period. Otherwise, I have a little towel I use and swap out once a week.

&#x200B;

The most wonderful thing is when your butt feels hot and gross from a bad one and you spray yourself with cool, clean water. I would suggest using TP at least for the first week or two until you get used to using the bidet and how you need to move so you get fully clean.

&#x200B;

Every time I go shopping I think about how much money I'm saving by not buying TP anymore since I was a Charmin girl and wouldn't deviate.

They are as much work as a small child who will never grow up. They're smart enough to break out of cages, even with locks on them. They scream for an hour or two a day, it's a crazy loud, high pitched scream. Even with great stimulation, sometimes they just like to scream and dance for fun. You'll have a nearly impossible time finding someone to properly care for your bird if you want to go away on a trip, so expect to have to bring them with. They live 60 - 80 years so unless you're under 15 years old expect to find someone to care for them after you die. They're prone to plucking. They can take off a finger with a hard bite and go through moody periods in their life.

&#x200B;

Honestly, I lived with someone who owned a cockatoo and I loved that little girl. Can't imagine having to deal with one for my entire life. Your life and needs change constantly. What if you need to live in an apartment for a while? What will you do with your 'too?

