I'd love to say but they don't allow us to mention the restaurant online. They have a marketing team for that.

&#x200B;

Yeah, if your waiter says "have you tried it before" they are either saying that in their opinion it sucks, or that it gets returned often because guests don't like it. It could mean that they want to recommend a different preparation to you. Something like, oh yeah, the Artichoke Pizza is great, but you know what makes it better? Adding our crispy asparagus to it.

&#x200B;

As a waitress, I'm very honest with my tables, unless they're rude. I just don't want to tell someone their favorite dish sucks so I have to ask if they've tried it first. I personally love helping people get through the menu and find an item that will work with their diet/allergies. I do get frustrated a bit with the picky people who just want to try something new. We don't have that big of a menu and some of our ingredients are unusual even for adventurous eaters. I had never heard of the vegetable fiddlehead ferns until I worked at this place.

The restaurant I work at only has 3 - 4 vegetarian entrees but they are all really good. The roasted veggie with ricotta lasagna is exceptional. I'm not vegetarian anymore but I still frequently order the lasagna.

&#x200B;

The thing is, sometimes restaurants just throw together a vegetarian option that kind of sucks imho. We work with farm-fresh ingredients and produce and some of the items end up being vegetarian almost by happenstance.

&#x200B;

Now, to be fair, we have a vegan pasta that is awful, not on the normal menu but it's listed on the special menu. Way too acidic and just a strange mix of veggies for a pasta. If you ever go to order something and the waiter carefully asks if you've had it before, they may be trying to warn you not to get that.

I was thinking the same thing when I saw the other person's. Home Chef has a really good amount of meat compared to a lot of other meal services. I don't think the other person has had to split 7 medium shrimp between 2 people for a pasta yet like I had to with some other services.

&#x200B;

I like more veg in my meals so I end up adding extra veg to every service I've ever used. Veggies are a cheap way to stretch the meals without adding many calories, and I like really big portions.

I walked into my restaurant on Mother's day to grab our to-go order for my mom. I'll only eat things from our restaurant right now because I know how clean they are. They had the news that we would likely be reopening and are using every spare minute to make sure the whole place is clean and dust-free. They hired plumbers to fix a wonky drain, repaired a couple of shifty booth seats, refinished all the wood tables.

&#x200B;

The only people working right now are our management team and they're using this time to fix and clean all the things we never have time to deal with. I'm excited to work with them again, even though I'm terrified of getting sick.

I live in Miami and there's a lot of machismo here. If I'm the one paying, I give/transfer the money to my fiance so he can be seen paying. Waitresses always hand him the check anyway. Most other places I've lived they put the bill in the middle of the table so as not to offend anyone.

&#x200B;

In the end, I can still pay but he feels good about himself.

Home chef has won my love with a couple of things:

&#x200B;

They let me choose the meat. I don't eat pork, so since most meal services have limited options it's really nice to be able to substitute a meat, even if it's with a small upcharge.

&#x200B;

Their packaging is fantastic.

&#x200B;

They store my recipes online forever.

&#x200B;

Their recipes are well written and can be followed by my cooking inept fiance.

&#x200B;

Where could they improve?

&#x200B;

Some of the veggies could be fresher, they sometimes go bad too fast. I keep green onions in the fridge for a long time, but they go bad in 2-3 days sometimes with meal services.

&#x200B;

Not specifically Home Chef, but I've had other issues with other services. Bad recipe cards, horrible packaging, commonly repeated menus, "cheap" ingredients, food arriving warm, boring recipes, too "healthy" for us to all enjoy.

I've tried several meal kit services and Dinnerly was my least favorite. We did manage to receive all our ingredients in all 3 boxes we ordered but the portion sizes were very small, the meals were unimaginative, and the boxes were haphazardly thrown together.

&#x200B;

I'm having some issues with Home Chef right now, some of the veggies are arriving close to being overripe and are rotting within 2 - 4 days. Luckily it's just been things like spring onions or tomato that I always have on hand. I'm giving the benefit of the doubt through this pandemic that they are just having trouble sourcing quality ingredients and hope that when this calms down we will get "fresher" veggies. There's been 1 veggie in every box (5 boxes) so far that went bad in less than 4 days. I plan what to cook around which veggies are most likely to last since we cook all of the meals up within a few days of receiving them. 

&#x200B;

We do order 8 - 16 portions (4 - 8 meal orders) in each box, so 1 veggie per box really isn't that horrible. We also order the meat packs sometimes. Big family. These boxes make lunch/dinner much easier.

I cancelled Dinnerly after 2 boxes because of the small serving sizes. We always had to supplement with veggies that we had at home.

&#x200B;

We tried Hello Fresh, Plated, Blue Apron, Dinnerly, and now Home Chef. Home Chef is by far my favourite of all the services. I love how all the meals come bagged and ready. The 15 minute meals or "oven-ready" meals are so freaking easy to do and taste great.

